# File structure

Once unpacked from the distribution format, a WebExtension is a directory containing a number of files.

Note: In some operating systems, filenames are case insensitive. This can lead to naming collisions.

## manifest.json

A <a href="#manifest">Manifest</a> file.

## _locales subdirectory

An optional directory containing strings as defined in <a href="#localization">localization</a>.

## Other files

An extension may also contain other files, such as those referenced in the [[#key-content_scripts]] and [[#key-background]] parts of the [=manifest=].

# Manifest

A WebExtension must have a <dfn>manifest</dfn> file at its root directory.

## Manifest file

A manifest file is a [[!JSON]] document named `manifest.json`. Malformed JSON files are not supported. Note that some implementors may accept comments, represented by any content following `//` outside of a JSON string.

## Manifest keys

If manifest keys that are not defined in this specification are specified, implementors must ignore those keys.

If manifest keys that are defined in this specification are specified with a different JSON type than defined in this specification, implementors must ignore those keys.

The following keys must be considered valid:

* <a href="#key-manifest_version">`manifest_version`</a>: required.
* <a href="#key-name">`name`</a>: required.
* <a href="#key-version">`version`</a>: required.
* <a href="#key-default_locale">`default_locale`</a>: required under some conditions.
* <a href="#key-background">`background`</a>: optional
* <a href="#key-commands">`commands`</a>: optional
* <a href="#key-content_scripts">`content_scripts`</a>: optional
* <a href="#key-content_security_policy">`content_security_policy`</a>: optional
* <a href="#key-description">`description`</a>: optional
* <a href="#key-icons">`icons`</a>: optional
* <a href="#key-optional_permissions">`optional_permissions`</a>: optional
* <a href="#key-options_ui">`options_ui`</a>: optional
* <a href="#key-permissions">`permissions`</a>: optional
* <a href="#key-short_name">`short_name`</a>: optional
* <a href="#key-web_accessible_resources">`web_accessible_resources`</a>: optional
* <a href="#key-devtools_page">`devtools_page`</a>: optional
* <a href="#key-externally_connectable">`externally_connectable`</a>: optional
* <a href="#key-trial_tokens">`trial_tokens`</a>: optional

The following keys must be considered valid in Manifest V3:

* <a href="#key-host_permissions">`host_permissions`</a>: optional
* <a href="#key-optional_host_permissions">`optional_host_permissions`</a>: optional

### Key `manifest_version`

This key must be present.

### Key `name`

Name of the extension used in the browser’s user interface. This should be the full name used to identify the extension. See also <a href="#key-short_name">`short_name`</a>.

This key must be present. This property can be localized.

### Key `version`

This key must be present.

### Key `permissions`

This key may be present. Its value is a [=list=] of [=strings=] that name the permissions the extension requests. The user agent ignores a string that is not the name of a permission it recognizes, including a string that differs from a name only in case.

Issue: Further specify the `permissions` key. See [issue 1150](https://github.com/w3c/webextensions/issues/1150).

### Key `optional_permissions`

This key may be present. Its value is a [=list=] of [=strings=] that name permissions the extension can request while it runs.

Issue: Further specify the `optional_permissions` key. See [issue 1150](https://github.com/w3c/webextensions/issues/1150).

### Key `host_permissions`

This key may be present.

### Key `optional_host_permissions`

This key may be present.

### Key `default_locale`

This key must be present if the `_locales` subdirectory is present, must be absent otherwise.

### Key `background`

This key may be present.

Issue: Specify background scripts. For relevant discussion, see https://github.com/w3c/webextensions/issues/282

### Key `commands`

This key may be present.

### Key `content_scripts`

The <a href="#key-content_scripts">`content_scripts`</a> key is a [=list=] of items representing [=content scripts=] that should be registered.

### Key `content_security_policy`

This key may be present.

### Key `description`

This key may be present.

### Key `icons`

This key may be present.

### Key `options_ui`

This key may be present.

### Key `short_name`

The short name of the extension. This value should be used in contexts where <a href="#key-name">`name`</a> is too long to use in full. If `short_name` is not provided, manifest consumers should use a truncated version of `name`.

This key may be present. This property can be localized.

### Key `web_accessible_resources`

This key may be present.

### Key `externally_connectable`

The <a href="#key-externally_connectable">`externally_connectable`</a> key declares which extensions and web pages can establish connections to the extension using [=runtime.connect()=] and [=runtime.sendMessage()=]. If omitted, all extensions may connect, but no web pages can connect.

A call to [=runtime.connect()=] from an external web page or extension triggers the [=runtime.onConnectExternal=] event listener to fire. Similarly, a message sent by an external web page or extension with [=runtime.sendMessage()=] triggers the [=runtime.onMessageExternal=] event listener to fire. These events are only available in a [=privileged extension context=], not in web pages or less-privileged contexts like [=content scripts=].

This key may be present and may include the following optional keys:

#### Key `ids` #### {#key-externally_connectable-ids}

A [=list=] of [=extension IDs=] that specifies which extensions can communicate with the extension. To allow all extensions to connect, include the wildcard pattern `"*"`.

Note: The default of whether an extension can connect changes based on whether the `externally_connectable` key is present. If the key is omitted, the default is to allow all extensions to connect. If the key is present, no extensions can connect unless specified in `"ids"`, even if the `"ids"` key is ommitted.

#### Key `matches` #### {#key-externally_connectable-matches}

A [=list=] of [=match patterns=] that specifies which web pages can communicate with the extension. If left empty or omitted, no web pages can connect.

Note: Only the document's own URL is used for matching. There is no fallback to the URL of the document that created it.

### Key `devtools_page`

This key may be present.

### Key `trial_tokens`

The <a href="#key-trial_tokens">`trial_tokens`</a> key is an optional [=list=] of strings. These may be used by the user agent to enable experimental features for the [[#extension-origin]]. The user agent may ignore or warn about any unrecognized, expired or malformed keys but any such entries must not prevent the extension from loading.

## Reserved file names

Filenames beginning with an underscore (`_`) are reserved for use by user agent.

# Execution contexts

Extensions can execute JavaScript code, in any of the following execution contexts:

* An <dfn>extension context</dfn> is a [=realm=] associated with an [=extension origin=].
* A <dfn>privileged extension context</dfn> is an [=extension context=] with access to the full set of extension APIs available to the extension. For example, the background page or worker defined through [[#key-background]].
* A <dfn>content script context</dfn> is an [=extension context=] and [=isolated world=] with limited access to a subset of extension APIs. This is the default execution environment of all [=content scripts=] of an extension.
* The [=main world=] of a web page not associated with an [=extension origin=] is not an extension context. It does not have access to any extension API, except when an extension allows so through [[#key-externally_connectable]].

Some extension APIs may involve the execution of JavaScript code in contexts other than what is specified above. For example, the `userScripts` API allows the creation of `USER_SCRIPT` worlds that are isolated similarly to [=isolated worlds=] but with distinct API availability.

## Isolated worlds

A <dfn>world</dfn> is a [=realm=] with its own [=global object=].

The <dfn>main world</dfn> is the [=realm=] whose [=global object=] is the
associated document's {{Window}}, in which the document's own scripts run.
This is the realm implied throughout other specifications that assume a single
realm per document.

A document may also have a number of [=isolated worlds=], created by the user
agent to run [=content scripts=] in a [=content script context=].

An <dfn>isolated world</dfn> is a distinct [=realm=] whose [=global object=]'s
interface is {{Window}}, associated with the [=main world=]'s document. The
[=platform objects=] of this realm are distinct from their counterparts in the
[=main world=], but operate on the same underlying state. These operations
should maintain isolation across realms: no object in an [=isolated world=]'s
realm is observable from the [=main world=].

<div class="note">
For example, {{CustomEvent}} specifies the `event.detail` attribute that
"must return the value it was initialized to". Event dispatch can cross worlds,
and following this requirement to the letter would result in the exposure of
an object from one realm to another. Potential resolutions include:

- Throwing at `CustomEvent` construction when a non-primitive value is passed.
- Returning `null`.
- Returning a structured clone, per [[HTML#safe-passing-of-structured-data]].
- Returning a redacted version of the object.

</div>

# Unavailable APIs

# The `browser` global

[[webextensions-browser-global inline]] is the primary namespace hosting extension APIs, available to [=extension contexts=].

Although the [=main world=] of a web page is not an [=extension context=], it may also contain the `browser` global to offer access to functionality granted by [[#key-externally_connectable]].

# <dfn>Extension origin</dfn>

The extension origin is a [=tuple origin=] consisting of an [=extension scheme=] and an extension-specific host. An <dfn>extension scheme</dfn> is a browser-specific scheme reserved for extension use.

<div class="example">
Examples of [=extension schemes=] include `chrome-extension`, `moz-extension`, and `safari-web-extension`.
</div>

# Localization

The _locales subdirectory of a WebExtension can contain strings for internationalization purposes.

Issue(62): Specify localization handling.

# Host permissions

## Cross-origin fetch

# Match patterns

A <dfn>match pattern</dfn> is a pattern used to match URLs. They are case-insensitive.

# Globs

A <dfn>glob</dfn> can be any [=string=]. It can contain any number of wildcards where `*` can match zero or more characters and `?` matches exactly one character.

# Concepts

## Uniqueness of extension IDs

## Promises and callbacks

## Tabs and windows

A <dfn>tab</dfn> is a [=top-level traversable=] that belongs to a browser window. Each browser window has a <dfn>selected tab</dfn>, the tab that it currently shows.

Note: Extension popups, sidebars and side panels are not tabs.

## User gestures and activeTab

Issue: Specify which user interactions give grant a user gesture, how long the gesture lasts, and whether it passes from one context to another. See [issue 918](https://github.com/w3c/webextensions/issues/918), [issue 919](https://github.com/w3c/webextensions/issues/919), and [issue 920](https://github.com/w3c/webextensions/issues/920).

The <dfn>activeTab permission</dfn> is the permission named `"activeTab"`, which an extension lists in [[#key-permissions]] or [[#key-optional_permissions]]. An extension has the [=activeTab permission=] if it lists the permission in [[#key-permissions]], or if it lists the permission in [[#key-optional_permissions]] and the user agent has granted the extension's request for it, such as through `permissions.request()`.

Issue: Specify whether a grant ends when the extension loses the [=activeTab permission=], such as through `permissions.remove()`.

When the user invokes an extension that has the [=activeTab permission=], the user agent runs the steps in [[#granting-the-activetab-permission]], which can give the extension an [=activeTab grant=] for a [=tab=]. The grant gives the extension access to the tab for the purposes in [[#access-from-an-activetab-grant]], until it ends as described in [[#end-of-an-activetab-grant]].

A <dfn>document in a tab</dfn> is a [=Document/fully active=] [=document=] whose [=node navigable=] has a [=tab=] as its [=navigable/top-level traversable=].

### Invoking an extension

An <dfn>invocation</dfn> of an extension is one of the following user interactions, each of which identifies a [=tab=], called the <dfn for="invocation">target tab</dfn>. No other user interaction is an invocation.

* Clicking the extension's action, a button that the user agent shows for the extension, such as a toolbar button. The target tab is the [=selected tab=] of the browser window in which the user activates the action.
* Choosing a menu item that the extension created with the `menus` or `contextMenus` API, in a menu that the user agent shows for a tab, for a [=document in a tab=], or for the extension's action. The target tab is that tab, the tab of that document, or, for a menu shown for the action, the [=selected tab=] of the browser window that contains the action.
* Pressing the keyboard shortcut of a command that the extension declares, including a shortcut that activates the extension's action, while a browser window is focused. The target tab is the [=selected tab=] of the focused window.
* Accepting input in the address bar while the extension's `omnibox` keyword is active, which dispatches `omnibox.onInputEntered`. The target tab is the [=selected tab=] of the browser window that contains the address bar.

Note: A call that the extension makes itself, such as `action.openPopup()`, is not an [=invocation=].

Issue: User agents differ on whether a call to `action.openPopup()` made while the extension has a user gesture is an invocation. This specification does not treat it as one. See [issue 1149](https://github.com/w3c/webextensions/issues/1149).

Issue: No user agent treats showing the extension's developer tools panel as an invocation, though [issue 386](https://github.com/w3c/webextensions/issues/386), which user agents support, proposes making it one.

### Granting the activeTab permission

An <dfn>activeTab grant</dfn> is access to a [=tab=] that an extension receives when the user invokes it. Each grant is for one extension and one tab, and has an [=origin=], the <dfn for="activeTab grant">origin of the grant</dfn>, which is the origin of the tab's active document when the grant is given. An extension has at most one activeTab grant for each tab, and a grant for one tab does not affect the extension's grants for other tabs.

When an [=invocation=] of an extension that has the [=activeTab permission=] occurs, the user agent runs the following steps before it dispatches any event that the invocation causes, such as `action.onClicked`:

1. Let |tab| be the [=invocation/target tab=].
1. Let |origin| be the [=Document/origin=] of |tab|'s [=navigable/active document=].
1. If |origin| is an [=opaque origin=], return.
1. If the user has denied the extension access to |origin|, or if |tab|'s active document is one that the user agent does not let extensions access, the user agent may return.
1. Give the extension an activeTab grant for |tab|, with |origin| as the [=activeTab grant/origin of the grant=], replacing any grant that the extension already has for |tab|.

A grant does not let the extension run scripts in a document that the user agent does not let extensions access, such as a document that is part of the user agent's own user interface.

### Access from an activeTab grant

While an extension has an [=activeTab grant=] for a [=tab=], the user agent treats the extension as having a host permission for the [=activeTab grant/origin of the grant=] for the following purposes:

* Injecting scripts and style sheets into any [=document in a tab|document=] in the granted tab whose origin is [=same origin=] with the [=activeTab grant/origin of the grant=], and removing the style sheets that the extension inserted, using the `scripting` API.
* Reading the URL and title of the tab's [=navigable/active document=] and the URL of the tab's favicon, such as through the `url`, `title` and `favIconUrl` properties of the `tabs.Tab` object that represents the tab.
* Capturing the visible content of the tab while it is the [=selected tab=] of its browser window, such as with `tabs.captureVisibleTab()`.
* Intercepting network requests in the tab to URLs whose origin is [=same origin=] with the [=activeTab grant/origin of the grant=], such as with `webRequest` and `declarativeNetRequest`.
* Making requests from a [=privileged extension context=], such as with `fetch()`, to URLs whose origin is [=same origin=] with the [=activeTab grant/origin of the grant=].

Issue: User agents differ on whether a grant lets the extension intercept network requests in the tab to the [=activeTab grant/origin of the grant=]. This specification allows it.

Issue: User agents differ on whether a grant lets the extension make requests to the [=activeTab grant/origin of the grant=] from its privileged extension contexts. This specification allows it. See the discussion of [PR 799](https://github.com/w3c/webextensions/pull/799).

A grant does not add to the permissions that the user agent has granted to the extension. For example, `permissions.contains()` does not report the grant, and the `cookies` API does not treat the extension as having a host permission for the [=activeTab grant/origin of the grant=].

Issue: User agents differ on whether an invocation runs [=content scripts=] that the extension declares in [[#key-content_scripts]] when the user has not given the extension access to that URL. See [issue 657](https://github.com/w3c/webextensions/issues/657).

### End of an activeTab grant

The user agent removes an extension's [=activeTab grant=] for a [=tab=] when any of the following occurs:

* The tab's [=navigable/active document=] becomes a document whose [=Document/origin=] is not [=same origin=] with the [=activeTab grant/origin of the grant=].
* The tab is closed.
* The extension is disabled, uninstalled, or reloaded.

A navigation of the tab to a document whose origin is same origin with the [=activeTab grant/origin of the grant=], including a reload, keeps the grant. So does a navigation that does not change the tab's active document, such as a navigation to a fragment, and a navigation of a [=child navigable=] in the tab. Navigating back to a document after the grant was removed does not restore the grant.

Issue: User agents differ on whether a grant ends when the tab navigates to a document whose origin is same origin with the [=activeTab grant/origin of the grant=], including a reload. This specification keeps the grant. See [issue 55](https://github.com/w3c/webextensions/issues/55).

Issue: User agents differ on whether a document that the user agent restores from the back/forward cache regains a grant that ended when the tab navigated away from it. This specification does not restore the grant. See [issue 55](https://github.com/w3c/webextensions/issues/55).

The user agent may also remove a grant when the user changes the extension's access to the [=activeTab grant/origin of the grant=], or when the tab's active document is one that the user agent does not let extensions access. A grant has no time limit.

